# Diagonalization, the single argument behind Cantor's theorem, the undecidability of the halting problem, and Godel's
# incompleteness, modeled by the kernel. The idea: given any list of functions, build the DIAGONAL function that disagrees
# with the n-th function at the n-th point. The diagonal cannot be anywhere in the list, because it differs from every row
# on the diagonal. So no list captures all functions, no program decides all halting questions, and no consistent formal
# system strong enough for arithmetic proves all true statements about itself. This file shows the diagonal escaping a
# concrete two-row table (using refutation, the two values are provably distinct), and the counting seed 2^n > n that makes
# the power set always strictly larger than the set.

form bit
  case off
  case on

task flip
  take b, like bit
  like bit
  fork case, read b
    case off
      send back
        make on
    case on
      send back
        make off

# Two functions to enumerate: the identity and the flip. A table of two rows, row off and row on.
task row-off
  take x, like bit
  like bit
  send back
    read x

task row-on
  take x, like bit
  like bit
  send back
    call flip
      read x

# The DIAGONAL function: at each point, flip the value the corresponding row gives there. d(off) = flip(row-off(off)),
# d(on) = flip(row-on(on)). By construction it disagrees with row n at point n.
task diagonal
  take x, like bit
  like bit
  fork case, read x
    case off
      send back
        call flip
          call row-off
            make off
    case on
      send back
        call flip
          call row-on
            make on

# THE DIAGONAL ESCAPES ROW OFF: at the point off, the diagonal differs from row-off, so the diagonal is not row-off. Proved
# by refutation, the two values are distinct constructors.
rule diagonal-differs-from-row-off
  show miss
    call is-equal
      call diagonal
        make off
      call row-off
        make off
  calm miss

# THE DIAGONAL ESCAPES ROW ON: at the point on, the diagonal differs from row-on, so the diagonal is not row-on. With the
# above, the diagonal equals NEITHER row, yet it is a perfectly good function: the list cannot contain every function.
rule diagonal-differs-from-row-on
  show miss
    call is-equal
      call diagonal
        make on
      call row-on
        make on
  calm miss

# THE COUNTING SEED, Cantor's theorem in finite form: the power set is strictly LARGER than the set, 2^n > n, so no map
# from a set onto its power set exists. This is why the diagonal always escapes.
hold power-set-exceeds-set-at-zero
  call is-above
    code 1
    code 0

hold power-set-exceeds-set-at-one
  call is-above
    code 2
    code 1

hold power-set-exceeds-set-at-two
  call is-above
    code 4
    code 2

hold power-set-exceeds-set-at-three
  call is-above
    code 8
    code 3

# The gap WIDENS: at n the power set has 2^n elements against n, and the difference only grows (8 against 3, then 16
# against 4), so the escape is permanent, not a small-case accident.
hold power-set-gap-widens
  call is-above
    code 16
    code 4
